          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong polynomial monad
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            whose underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunctor"} endofunctor
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+functor"} polynomial functor
            and whose unit and multiplication are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+natural+transformations"} cartesian natural transformations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            of polynomial functors and cartesian transformations.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            A basic example is the free-monoid monad (
            %a{:href => "#GambinoKock09"}> Gambino-Kock 09, Example 1.9
            ). This is exhibited by the polynomial
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ←
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                      %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1\leftarrow \mathbb{N}^\prime\rightarrow  \mathbb{N}\rightarrow 1
            where the middle arrow is such that for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\in \mathbb{N}
            its fiber over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            has cardinality
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p One can construct the free monad on a polynomial endofunctor, and it is a polynomial monad.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#parametric_right_adjointness Parametric right adjointness
          %p
            Every polynomial monad is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p.r.a.+monad"} p.r.a. monad
          %h3#cartesianness Cartesianness
          %p
            Since all polynomial functors preserve
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullbacks"} pullbacks
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monad"} cartesian monad
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi W
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Z
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                          %mo ′
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                          %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp; &amp; X &amp; \to &amp; Y\\
                  \&amp; \swarrow &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; \searrow \\
                  W &amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp; Z \\
                  \&amp; \nwarrow &amp; &amp; &amp; &amp; \nearrow \\
                  \&amp; &amp; X' &amp; \to &amp; Y'
                  }
          %p in which the middle square is a pullback. Thus, all the data of a polynomial monad can be described very concretely. This gives a convenient way to produce cartesian monads, and indeed very many cartesian monads arising in practice are in fact polynomial. Polynomial monads have close relations with a number of other notions.
          %h3#relation_to_clubs Relation to clubs
          %p
            Polynomial monads (on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            rather than a slice of it) are, in some sense, the most natural base monads for the theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/clubs"} clubs
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sieve"} sieve
            with respect to cartesian transformations. Moreover, the club monoidal structure can be seen as just an instance of the explicit construction of composition for polynomials (see 1.11 of (
            %a{:href => "#GambinoKock09"}> Gambino-Kock
            )).
          %h3#relation_to_generalized_multicategories Relation to generalized multicategories
          %p
            The bicategory of polynomial functors and cartesian transformations is in fact the horizontal bicategory of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi Poly
                      %mo ̲
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{Poly}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and whose cells are diagrams like
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi W
                        %mtd
                          %mo ←
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Z
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi W
                          %mo ′
                        %mtd
                          %mo ←
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                          %mo ′
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                          %mo ′
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Z
                          %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  W &amp; \leftarrow &amp; X &amp; \to &amp; Y &amp; \to &amp; Z \\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp;\downarrow\\
                  W' &amp; \leftarrow &amp; X' &amp; \to &amp; Y' &amp; \to &amp; Z'
                  }
          %p
            in which the
            %em middle
            square (only) is a pullback. Suppose
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo ′
                    %mo =
                    %mi Z
                    %mo ′
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W'=Z'=1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ′
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g':X'\to Y'
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo =
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W=Z
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y\to Z
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                        %mo ′
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X' \times_{Y'} Y \to Z
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Z
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y\to \Pi_{g'}(Z\times X')
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T Z
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is the polynomial functor determined by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g'
          %p
            Thus, a polynomial equipped with a cartesian transformation to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ←
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \leftarrow X' \to Y' \to 1
            is exactly a “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            \-span” such as arises in the theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+multicategories"} generalized multicategories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is a polynomial monad, then the slice double category of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mi Poly
                    %mo ̲
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{Poly}
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g'
            is equivalent to the double category of
            = succeed "-spans" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "-multicategories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            itself, rather than any slice, are the
            = succeed "-clubs" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "-multicategory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ←
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T X_0 \leftarrow X_1 \to X_0
            is identified with the monad it induces on
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C/X_0
          %h3#relation_to_object_classifiers Relation to object classifiers
          %p
            Polynomial monads have a natural interpretation in terms of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifiers"} object classifiers
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ←
                    %mi B
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi g
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \leftarrow B \xrightarrow{g} A \to 1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi g
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_{1,g,1}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_g
            of all pullbacks of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Id
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id \to P_{1,g,1}
            says that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_1 : 1\to 1
            is a pullback of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_{1,g,1} \circ P_{1,g,1}
            involves the classifying object for a pair of composable
            = succeed "-morphisms," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_g
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_{1,g,1} \circ P_{1,g,1} \to P_{1,g,1}
            tells us that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_g
            is closed under composition.
          %p
            Thus, a polynomial monad (on
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \cong C/1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            together with a coherent way to make
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_g
            into a category. More precisely, consider the slice category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cart
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cart(C)/g
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cart
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cart(C)
            is the category whose objects are morphisms of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and whose morphisms are pullback squares. This comes with source and target functors
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cart
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi g
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cart(C)/g \rightrightarrows C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_{1,g,1}
            into a polynomial monad is then equivalent to giving unit and composition functors enhancing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cart
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi g
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cart(C)/g \rightrightarrows C
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
            such that the forgetful map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cart
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi g
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cart
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cart(C)/g \to Cart(C)
            is a double functor (Theorem 5.3.3 of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Weber15"} Weber 2015
          %p
            For example, consider the free monoid monad above determined by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                      %mo ′
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:\mathbb{N}\prime \to \mathbb{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            is to say that its fibers are finite and have been equipped with bijections to canonical finite sets, which we may as well think of as giving them linear orders. To give the monad structure on this functor is equivalent to noting that every identity map has ordered finite fibers, and the composite of functions with ordered finite fibers again has ordered finite fibers, in a way that is associative, unital, and stable under pullback.
          %p Other interesting examples include:
          %ul
            %li
              The monad for symmetric strict monoidal categories on
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_g
              consists of discrete (op)fibrations with ordered finite fibers in which all reindexing functions are bijections.
            %li
              The monad for cartesian strict monoidal categories on
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_g
              consists of discrete fibrations with ordered finite fibers.
            %li
              The monad for cocartesian strict monoidal categories on
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_g
              consists of discrete opfibrations with ordered finite fibers.
          %p
            The class of discrete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Conduche+functors"} Conduche functors
            with ordered finite fibers is
            %em almost
            an example; it wants to be classified by the bicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FinSpan
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinSpan
            of spans between finite sets, but that is not an object of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            determined by a discrete Conduche functor with ordered finite fibers is a reasonable substitute for the nonexistent notion of “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FinSpan
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinSpan
            \-club”.
          %p
            Identifying the classes of morphisms corresponding to standard polynomial monads like these also tells us how to identify when a polynomial monad is induced by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/club"} club
            (or more generally a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+multicategory"} generalized multicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            for that monad belongs to the appropriate class, compatibly. For instance, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is the monad for cartesian strict monoidal categories, then a polynomial monad on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            is an
            = succeed "-club" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            in its defining polynomial is a discrete fibration with ordered finite fibers, and its composition and unit respect that structure.
          %h2#references References
          %p A fairly comprehensive discussion of the notion is due to
          %ul#GambinoKock09
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicola+Gambino"}> Nicola Gambino
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joachim+Kock"} Joachim Kock
              = succeed "," do
                %em Polynomial functors and polynomial monads
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0906.4931"} arXiv:0906.4931
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+monad"} algebras
            over polynomial monads is in
          %ul#BataninBerger13
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Batanin"}> Michael Batanin
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clemens+Berger"} Clemens Berger
              %em Homotopy theory for algebras over polynomial monads
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1305.0086"}> arXiv:1305.0086
              )
          %p
            An application to the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opetopes"} opetopes
            in discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joachim+Kock"}> Joachim Kock
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"} André Joyal
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Batanin"} Michael Batanin
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Fran%C3%A7ois+Mascari"} Jean-François Mascari
              (2010); Polynomial functors and opetopes; Advances in Mathematics, vol 224, pages 2690–2737,
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0706.1033"} arXiv:0706.1033
              %p#KJBM , §4.3-4.6, where the main result is that the polynomial monad of stable opetopes is a least fixpoint of the Baez-Dolan construction for polynomial monads.
          %p
            An extension of type theory with a universe of associative and unital polynomial monads to define
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opetopic+type+theory"} opetopic types
            in order to encode fully coherent algebraic structures is in
          %ul
            %li#AlliouxFinsterSozeau20
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Antoine+Allioux"}> Antoine Allioux
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric+Finster"} Eric Finster
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthieu+Sozeau"} Matthieu Sozeau
              %em Types are internal infinity-groupoids
              (
              %a{:href => "https://hal.inria.fr/hal-03133144/document"}> PDF
              )
          %p Polynomial monads as a “generalization of small categories” for homotopy theory are studied in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Batanin"}> Michael Batanin
              , Florian De Leger;
              = succeed "," do
                %em Grothendieck’s homotopy theory, polynomial monads and delooping of spaces of long knots
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.00904"} arxiv
          %p The relation between polynomial monads, object classifiers, and models of (homotopy) type theory is studied in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"}> Steve Awodey
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Natural models of homotopy type theory
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.3219"} arxiv
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"}> Steve Awodey
                , Clive Newstead;
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Polynomial pseudomonads and dependent type theory
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.00997"} arxiv
            %li
              %p
                Clive Newstead,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebraic models of dependent type theory
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.06155"} arxiv
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"}> Steve Awodey
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebraic type theory
                %a{:href => "https://hott.github.io/HoTT-2023/slides/awodey.pdf"} slides from HoTT 2023
          %p
            The presentation of polynomial monads on a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            with pullbacks in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+double+categories"} strict double categories
            is in Theorem 5.3.3 of:
          %ul
            %li#Weber2015
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mark+Weber"}> Mark Weber
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Polynomials in categories with pullbacks
              = succeed ":16" do
                %strong 30
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/30/16/30-16abs.html"} journal
          %p
            This data was called
            %strong bagdomain data
            in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+T.+Johnstone"}> Peter T. Johnstone
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Variations on the bagdomain theme
        .revisedby
